### Phase 3: QC Gate (review + validation, loops back to dev)

The quality gate. Two checks run here; either one failing sends the code back to `dev`, then the gate re-runs. **Max 3 fix rounds**: every `dev` round after a review counts, whatever it fixes. This is the only quality gate — the code is reviewed here, before the PR, so the PR is clean and there is no post-PR review round.

Before each review, snapshot the tree it will see:
`git -C <WT> add -A && git -C <WT> write-tree` → `<REVIEWED_TREE>`. The next round's delta is
`git -C <WT> add -A && git -C <WT> diff --cached <REVIEWED_TREE>`.

**Check 1 — code review.** Round 1 reviews the whole change. When it spans several modules or more
than about 1,500 changed lines, split it by module over parallel `review` agents in ONE message,
each with its own file list: small contexts cost less than one large one. Spawn the **`review`**
subagent (local-diff mode):

> <PROJECT_RULES_BLOB>
>
> Review ONLY these changed files (local diff, not a PR): <LIST OF CHANGED FILES>.
> They live in the worktree `<WT>` — read them there and diff with
> `git -C <WT> diff` (not the user's main checkout `src/{{ custom_repo_name }}`).
> Your goal here is COVERAGE: report every issue you find — including low-severity
> ones and ones you are unsure about — each tagged with a severity (CRITICAL /
> MAJOR / MINOR) and a confidence (high / medium / low). Cover TEST COVERAGE too:
> flag missing tests for computed fields, constraints, state transitions, access
> rights, error branches, and the regression case (old behavior still works).
> Do not self-filter; this orchestrator is the ranking/filter stage.
> For every new or changed test answer the three test-validity questions
> (reaches the branch / fails on the pre-fix code / assertions can fail).
> Behaviour this task changes on purpose: <ONE LINE EACH FROM THE ALIGNMENT
> SUMMARY, or "none">. A weakened existing test outside that list is CRITICAL.
> Every CRITICAL / MAJOR carries a failure scenario and one VERIFY line —
> the verification pass runs on those lines.
> review is read-only; your FILES_CHANGED will be "none (read-only)".
> Return OPEN_THREADS for anything worth filing separately, and DECISIONS for
> any judgment calls on severity.

The `review` agent has `style` + `testing` preloaded. It is read-only.

From round 2 the review gets the round delta, not the whole change again (round-delta mode):

> <PROJECT_RULES_BLOB>
>
> Round-delta review in the worktree `<WT>`. The diff since the last review:
> `git -C <WT> add -A && git -C <WT> diff --cached <REVIEWED_TREE>`. Re-check
> these findings and say for each fixed / still open / regressed: <IDS + ONE
> LINE EACH>. Report new issues in the delta with severity and confidence;
> a test the reviewed tree had and the delta weakens is CRITICAL unless its
> behaviour is on the round-1 list: <THAT LIST, or "none">.
> Read unchanged code only where a changed line depends on it. Every CRITICAL /
> MAJOR carries a failure scenario and one VERIFY line. Closing block as usual.

**Check 1b — verification pass (Critical / Major only).** For every line of the
review's `VERIFY:` block spawn one **`verify`** subagent — all in ONE message so
they run in parallel (cap 6: verify Criticals first, then Majors by the
review's confidence, high first; carry the rest as PLAUSIBLE). Prompt = the VERIFY line verbatim (id, file:line, claim, failure
scenario, decisive lines) + "the code is in the worktree `<WT>`". Read the
first-line verdicts:

- **REFUTED** → drop from the gate; list it in the gate report with the
  refuting line, so the author sees why it is gone.
- **PLAUSIBLE** → keep the severity, set confidence low, append the verifier's
  precondition to the finding.
- **CONFIRMED** → keep; the verifier's decisive lines go into the `dev` fix prompt.

Minor findings and Nits are not verified. A `verify` agent is read-only and
returns no closing block. The fix brief waits for every verdict.

**Check 2 — validation.** Run directly (NOT via subagent) — you need Chrome browser tools and this session's running Odoo. Everything here targets THIS session (`<CONF>` / `<DB>` / `<URL>`), never another session.

0. Backend checks run against THIS session from its conf — `{{ odoo_cmd }} shell -c <CONF> --no-http`
   with the check on stdin, or `psql -d <DB>` for plain reads — never over the Odoo
   connector, which is production.
1. Refresh the env (picks up any module added during dev) and stop its Odoo:
   ```bash
   bash "${{ project_dir_var }}/.claude/skills/worktree-env/scripts/wt-start.sh" <slug>
   systemctl --user stop odoo{{ service_suffix }}-wt@<slug>.service
   ```
   (only THIS session's Odoo stops; the user's `odoo{{ service_suffix }}.service` and other sessions
   keep running)
2. Update and run tests against this session:
   ```bash
   bash "${{ project_dir_var }}/.claude/skills/worktree-env/scripts/wt-test.sh" <slug> \
       -u <MODULE_NAME> --test-enable --test-tags /<MODULE_NAME> \
       > "${{ project_dir_var }}/.worktrees/_env/<slug>/test.log" 2>&1; \
     grep -E "odoo\.tests\.result|ERROR|CRITICAL|FAIL" "${{ project_dir_var }}/.worktrees/_env/<slug>/test.log"
   ```
   On a failure, `grep -n -A40 -E "^(FAIL|ERROR):|Traceback"` the log; the whole log never
   enters the context.
   `wt-test.sh` runs on a free port, so a browser tab left on `<URL>` by step 5
   of an earlier loop cannot send its requests into the tests' transaction; on
   the IPv4 loopback HttpCase talks to (the session listens on `::1`); and with
   a dbfilter naming `<DB>` (the session's `^%d$` maps `127.0.0.1` to no
