**Gate decision:**

- **BLOCKING** — any CONFIRMED or PLAUSIBLE CRITICAL review finding, any
  CONFIRMED MAJOR (a PLAUSIBLE Major and a REFUTED finding never block), OR a
  test/validation failure, OR a
  failed test case: report to the user, spawn a **fresh** `dev` agent to fix — never
  `SendMessage` the previous one (Agent economy). Its brief: `<PROJECT_RULES_BLOB>`,
  the "work in `<WT>`" instruction, the verified findings with their decisive lines,
  the Check 1 list of behaviour changed on purpose (the only tests it may edit),
  and `git -C <WT> diff --cached --stat <REVIEWED_TREE>` as what earlier rounds
  changed. Append its returns to the running lists, post a `Bug Fix` internal note,
  then **loop back and re-run this whole gate**.
- **STOP after 3 fix rounds** that still leave it blocking: report to the user what
  still blocks, what keeps coming back, and the choice — narrow the design, split
  the rest into a follow-up task, or more rounds. Recurring findings after three
  rounds point at the design, not at the last fix. With the pipeline-gates mod a
  fourth `dev` round is refused until the user types
  `/pipeline-gate open rounds <reason>` (three more).
- **NON-BLOCKING** — only PLAUSIBLE MAJOR / MINOR / low-confidence review findings: report
  them, add MINOR or low-confidence items to OPEN_THREADS instead of fixing them now
  ({% if use_kb %}the user decides on each in Phase 7{% else %}Phase 7 files them{% endif %}). Proceed.

Report: "QC gate: review <summary> · verified <n> (<c> confirmed / <p> plausible / <r> refuted) · tests <N> · test cases <X/Y> — PASS (after <k> fix loops)."
With the pipeline-gates mod, record the decision with `mcp__pipeline-gates__qc_verdict`: `pass`
with that report line, or `blocking` with the reason before the fix loop.

